'''ametropia''' (am-e-trō′pi-ä̤), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. ἄμετρος, irregular, + ὤψ (ὠπ-), eye.] A condition of the eye which is abnormal with respect to refraction: the opposite of ''emmetropia.'' It comprises myopia, hypermetropia, presbyopia, and astigmatism. See these words.

'''ametropic''' (am-e-trop′ik), ''a.'' Pertaining to or produced by ametropia.

'''ametrous''' (a-mē′trus), ''a.'' [< Gr. ἀ- priv. + μήτρα, uterus: see ''matrix.''] In ''teratol.'', without a uterus. ''Syd. Soc. Lex.''

'''ameve†''', ''v.'' Same as ''amove''<sup>1</sup>.

'''amgarn''' (am′gärn), ''n.'' [W. ''amgarn'', a ferrule.] In ''archæol.'', a kind of celt supposed to have served as the ferrule of a spear-shaft. {{smaller|Such celts commonly have a loop on one side, and in some instances a ring has been found passing through the loop.}}

'''Amharic''' (am-har′ik), ''n.'' [< ''Amhara'', the central division of Abyssinia.] The modern cultivated language of Abyssinia.
{{smaller block|''Amharic'' . . . has been since {{sc|a. d.}} 1300 the language of the Court and Nobles [of Abyssinia].
{{right|''R. N. Cust'', Mod. Langs. of Africa, p. 88.}}}}

'''Amherstia''' (am-hėrs′ti-ä̤), ''n.'' [NL., named in honor of Countess ''Amherst'', a zealous promoter of botany.] A leguminous arboreous genus of Burmese plants, of a single species, ''A. nobilis'', with very large flowers, bright vermilion spotted with yellow, in long pendulous racemes. {{smaller|The flowers are considered sacred, and are laid as an offering before the shrines of Buddha.}}

