'''Amia''' (am′i-ä̤), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. ἀμία, a kind of tunny (see def.); applied by Linnæus to an American genus.] '''1.''' A genus of ganoid fishes, typical of the family ''Amiidæ, Amia calva'' being the only extant species. {{smaller|It inhabits the fresh waters of North America, and is known as the bowfin, dogfish, mudfish, lawyer, brindle, grindle, and John A. Grindle. The fish known as amia to the ancients was a very different one. Also called ''Amiatus''. See cut under ''Amiidæ''.}} —'''2.''' A genus of acanthopterygian fishes: synonymous with ''Apogon''. ''Gronovius.''

'''amiability''' (ā″mi-ạ-bil′i-ti), ''n.'' [< ''amiable'': see ''-bility''. Cf. OF. ''amiableté''.] '''1.''' The quality of being amiable; excellence of disposition; amiableness.
{{smaller block|Suetonius mentions, as an instance of the ''amiability'' of Titus, that he was accustomed to jest with the people during the combats of the gladiators.
{{right|''Lecky'', Europ. Morals, I. 304.}}}}
'''2.''' Lovableness; amability. ''N. E. D.''

'''amiable''' (ā′mi-ạ-bl), ''a.'' [< ME. ''amiable, amyable, aimiable'', < OF. ''aimiable, amiable, amiaule'', amiable, lovely, friendly, < LL. ''amicabilis'' (> E. ''amicable''), friendly, < L. ''amicare'', make friendly, < ''amicus'', a friend, prop. an adj., friendly, loving, < ''amare'', love: see ''amor'', ''amour'', etc. The sense ‘lovable, lovely’ is due to a confusion with F. ''aimable'', OF. ''amable'', < L. ''amabilis'', lovable, lovely, < ''amare'', as above.] '''1.''' Friendly; kindly; amicable.
{{smaller block|Lay an ''amiable'' siege to the honesty of this Ford’s wife.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. W. of W., ii. 2.}}
That foreign eccentricity to which their nation is so ''amiable''. ''Howells'', A Foregone Conclusion, p. 72.}}
'''2.''' Exciting or tending to excite love or delight;

readings.c1s3:
lovable; lovely; beautiful; delightful; pleasing.

{{smaller block|How ''amiable'' are thy tabernacles, O Lord. Ps. lxxxiv. 1.}}

{{smaller block|No company can be more ''amiable'' than that of men of sense who are soldiers. {{right|''Steele'', Spectator, No. 152.}}}}

{{smaller block|I found my wife and daughter well, the latter grown quite a woman, with many ''amiable'' accomplishments acquired in my absence. {{right|''Franklin'', Autobiog., p. 314.}}}}

'''3.''' Specifically, possessing or exhibiting agreeable moral qualities, as sweetness of temper, kind-heartedness, and the like; having an excellent disposition: as, an ''amiable'' girl; an ''amiable'' disposition.

{{smaller block|This [word] and “lovely” have been so far differentiated that ''amiable'' never expresses now any other than moral loveliness; which in “lovely” is seldom or never implied. {{right|''Abp. Trench.''}}}}

{{smaller block|He is so ''amiable'' that you will love him, if ever you become acquainted with him. {{right|''Jefferson'', in Bancroft’s Hist. Const., II. 353.}}}}

{{smaller block|His [Fox’s] private friends . . . maintained that . . . if he was misled, he was misled by ''amiable'' feelings, by a desire to serve his friends and by anxious tenderness for his children. {{right|''Macaulay'', Lord Holland.}}}}

{{smaller block|='''Syn.''' Engaging, benignant, sweet-tempered, kind-hearted, lovely in character.}}

'''amiableness''' (ā′mi-a̤-bl-nes), ''n.'' The quality of being amiable; loveliness; amiability.

'''amiably''' (ā′mi-a̤-bli), ''adv.'' '''1.''' In an amiable manner; in a manner to excite or attract love. —'''2†.''' Pleasingly; delightfully. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|The palaces rise so ''amiably''. {{right|''Sir T. Herbert'', Travels, p. 129.}}}}

